The website mentions that I have an argument showing that
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1)^n\frac{1}{p_{n+1} - p_n}
\end{equation*}
is unbounded in at least one direction (i.e. positive or negative) assuming the Hardy-Littlewood prime $k$-tuples conjecture. In a comment in [[141]](https://www.erdosproblems.com/141), I pointed out that (assuming the prime $k$-tuples conjecture) if $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is admissible, then there are infinitely many translations of $A$ into consecutive primes. (EDIT: this result was actually originally due to Schinzel and Sierpinski.) So let $A$ be an admissible set that consists of pairs of numbers that differ by two. We can choose $A$ such that there are lots of pairs, and such that the pairs are spaced widely apart. By making the amount of pairs and the distances between pairs arbitrarily large, we can make it so the series changes by an arbitrarily large amount at every translation of $A$ into a set of consecutive primes. That completes the proof.
Unfortunately, the prime $k$-tuples conjecture doesn't give us a good way of controlling the index of the primes (i.e. odd or even), so it's not clear which direction the series must be unbounded in or whether it must be unbounded in both directions. Perhaps this could be explored assuming a uniform version of the prime $k$-tuples conjecture?

