Call such a set *Sidon with at most one exception* (very original name, I know), and let $F(N)$ be the largest size of such a set within $\{1,...,N\}$. Of course, Erdős and Freud have shown that $(\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}} + o(1))N^{1/2} \leq F(N)$.
I have a nice argument which shows that $F(N) \leq (\sqrt{2} + o(1))N^{1/2}$. If $A$ is Sidon with at most one exception, and $n$ is the unique number that can be represented as a sum in more than one way, then the sets $A \cap [1, n/2]$ and $A \cap (n/2, n]$ are individually Sidon. That is, if $A$ is Sidon with at most one exception, then $A$ can be split into two intervals on which it is Sidon. If we let $h(N)$ be the size of the largest Sidon set in $\{1,...,N\}$, then we therefore have
\begin{equation*}
F(N) \leq \max_k h(k) + h(N - k).
\end{equation*}
It is known (see [[30]](https://www.erdosproblems.com/30)) that $h(N) = N^{1/2} + o(N^{1/2})$. Using calculus, you can see that for fixed $N$, the value $k^{1/2} + (N - k)^{1/2}$ is never greater than $\sqrt{2}(N^{1/2})$. It follows that $F(N) \leq (\sqrt{2} + o(1))N^{1/2}$.

Nice! I was caught up trying to restrict the semiprimes in ways that satisfy the problem, but you're right - the entire set of semiprimes (excluding primes squared), together with the primes, has Property P.
On sets of squarefree numbers: if we let $f(n)$ be the size of the largest set with Property P in $\{1,...,n\}$, and we let $g(n)$ be the size of the largest such set consisting of squarefree numbers, then we have
\begin{equation*}
(1 + o(1))\frac{n \log \log n}{\log n} \leq g(n) \leq f(n) \leq n.
\end{equation*}
By partitioning $\{1,...,n\}$ according to the largest square factor of a number, we get
\begin{equation*}
f(n) \leq \sum_{k=1}^\infty g\left(\left\lfloor\frac{n}{k^2}\right\rfloor\right).
\end{equation*}
I'm not sure if the current bounds are good enough to do anything with this (for example, I don't currently see a way to prove that $f(n) \ll g(n)$), but I suspect that if you can find a ''nice enough'' asymptotic upper bound for $g(n)$, then it could be combined with the above inequality to give an upper bound for $f(n)$. Erdős does claim that Sárközy proved $f(n) = o(n)$, though I'm not sure if anyone knows of a reference. Perhaps it would be helpful here if one could find it.

Let $A$ be the set of all numbers of the form $pq$, where $p$ and $q$ are primes such that $\pi(p) \not\equiv \pi(q) \pmod{2}$. (That is, one is an odd-indexed prime and the other is an even-indexed prime.) Then $A$ has the desired property. Furthermore, $A$ (asymptotically) contains half the semiprimes, so we have
\begin{equation*}
|A| \geq (\frac{1}{2} + o(1))\frac{n \log \log n}{\log n}.
\end{equation*}

